From 9f2b0feacd456e1f6b2e73056c2e49b9baa4c9ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cary Palmer <24235924+professorpalmer@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 02:07:01 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] cuda: tiered KV prefill writes new rows through the VRAM
 alias, prefetches host rows

Past the VRAM line of a tiered KV cache, each prefill micro-batch wrote its new K/V rows into host memory with
the quantizing set_rows kernels, in small pieces over PCIe: a fixed ~0.68 ms per prefill token on an RTX 4070
(512-token micro-batches), the same at a 16k and a 119k line.

Graphs that run without CUDA graphs (prefill) now:
- point set_rows nodes that write into a tiered buffer at its all-VRAM alias, so the new host-part rows land in
  the VRAM staging buffer, and copy them back to their host pages with whole 16-byte stores (one block per row);
- copy the host rows of attention op k into staging on a second stream while the main stream computes the layers
  before op k; the main stream waits for that copy before the layer's set_rows writes staging.

Both read and write the same bytes as before. GGML_CUDA_KV_TIER_REDIRECT=0 / GGML_CUDA_KV_TIER_PREFETCH=0
disable the parts. Decode graphs (CUDA graphs) keep the synchronous staging.

RTX 4070, Bonsai 2 27B, q8_0 K/V, VRAM line pinned at 16k, prefill to 40k: 717 -> 983 tok/s (all-VRAM cache:
990); without the redirect 721, without the prefetch 967.
